# ⛔ ONE LINE ENDING: LF, IN THE OBJECT DATABASE AND IN EVERY CHECKOUT, ON EVERY OS (kb/Work PB3029; owner 2026-10-11:
# "We should probably standardize on a single line-ending format. CRLF vs others keeps biting us"). Git stores a text
# file LF and checks it out LF whatever `core.autocrlf` says (Git for Windows' system gitconfig sets it true), so a
# Windows gate and a Linux gate read the SAME bytes from the same commit. Before this rule every Windows checkout was
# CRLF, and two narrower rules here forced LF where a CR broke a tool outright: a CRLF'd shell script dies at its first
# `set -u\r` (kb/Work PB1732, wave 72 group G), and the Workflow tool refuses a script holding a CR as "control
# characters". A file git's heuristic calls binary (a NUL, or a lone CR) is stored as-is, so
# `scripts/line_endings.py --check` (a gate audit, and CI's) refuses a CR in any tracked TEXT file, in the index or in
# the checkout. A file that must keep its bytes is declared `-text` below, WITH ITS REASON. Nothing here must be CRLF
# (no .bat or .cmd is tracked; one would be declared `eol=crlf` here, with its reason).
* text=auto eol=lf

# The PINNED Unicode data under data/unicode/ is CONTENT-ADDRESSED: root-collation.manifest.json records the
# SHA-256 of each input file's BYTES, and CollationTableTests.Manifest_InputsAndOutput_MatchTheCommittedFiles
# re-hashes them to prove the committed table IS the generator's output over the committed sources. Under
# core.autocrlf=true a fresh Windows clone (or an agent worktree — observed 2026-08-18) checks them out CRLF,
# every input hash changes, and that drift test fails on a tree nobody edited; regenerating there would then
# commit CRLF hashes and break every LF checkout. Declared byte-exact so every checkout hashes what the
# generator read. The .txt glob also covers LICENSE-UNICODE.txt, which must stay byte-exact for the same reason.
data/unicode/*.txt -text
data/unicode/*.xml -text
data/unicode/cldr/** -text
tests/Cobol.Net.Tests.Unit/TestData/segmentation/*.txt -text

# The public-API baselines (Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers) are SETS of lines: two branches that each add a
# member append to the same place, which a line merge calls a conflict although the answer is always both lines. Train
# 1052 (2026-10-10) stopped on exactly that in PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt; merge=union takes both sides (`git merge`, which
# enqueue.py land uses to bring origin/main into a branch, honors it; kb/Work PB3023), and a stale line union keeps fails
# the build (RS0017), so the gate still sees a wrong set (kb/Work PB2885).
src/**/PublicAPI.*.txt merge=union

# A conformance fixture whose BYTES are its subject: CR LF terminates a free-form line, and a CR not followed by a LINE
# FEED is a character of the line (DOC-A.1-156, kb/Work PB1496), so it keeps its CR LF terminators and its lone CRs.
conformance-suite/cases/6.5-Logical-conversion/pb1496_lone_cr_is_a_character.cob -text
